From 15c81a3cb28172b1aabe00a3e3c3b26c41d0f347 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 05:24:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] ci: batch unowned CI flakes into one durable daily
 intake card

Why: on 2026-11-13 UTC the ci-flaky-cards producer created 35 cards (one per
first flake of each test id) and the cards spent 146 runs / 13.0 clipped
worker slot-hours (hub task_runs query, created_by=ci-flaky-cards). Card
fan-out per test id, not the fixes themselves, drives the cost.

What: new UNOWNED flake occurrences go into a durable SQLite queue
(<state>.intake.sqlite3) with full provenance. Each repo gets ONE intake card
per UTC day (idempotency key ci-flaky-intake:<owner/repo>:<YYYY-MM-DD>), and
immutable, size-bounded JSON manifest pages give every occurrence a
needs_triage status. Existing open exact-test/job owners, local or foreign,
still get deduplicated comments with readback. They are never reassigned or
archived. This is admission batching only: the red-main, retry, grace and
threshold policy is unchanged.

Durability: the queue needs an explicit one-time --initialize-intake, with a
SQLite UUID plus a separately retained identity anchor. Normal runs never
auto-create the queue, so losing or replacing it fails closed. Pages are
committed before any external write, ambiguous create/comment attempts stay
immutable, and an exact readback decides delivery. FIFO tickets rotate
retries fairly. A drain runs before discovery with half the budget. HTTP has
an absolute deadline across retries, pagination and signed artifact hops.
A done intake is reopened. An archived intake carries forward to the next UTC
day. Closed-foreign-owner history is used only when the replica's comment
count is complete and nothing is clipped. A clipped closed candidate no
longer strands the row: the row is admitted with an owner_check entry that
names the candidate for triage.

Ownership coverage contract (docs/ci-flaky-intake.md): no-match absence is
checked against this board plus fresh hub-replica scopes. That is not a
global certificate; unsynced legacy profile boards are not inventoried
(same exposure as the baseline create-overlap guard). The intake card owns
final dedupe.

Proof:
- Spec cases (CLI, isolated HOME/HERMES_HOME, fake Board/hub effects):
  30 distinct occurrences => 1 card + 30 durable rows; same-run replay =>
  no duplicate; existing local/foreign owner => comment+readback; failed
  delivery stays retryable; UTC rollover; state loss/recovery; pagination
  keeps counts; closed intake reopens or carries forward.
- Historical replay (read-only capture of the metadata artifacts for all 32
  source runs behind the 35 cards created 2026-11-13; 35 occurrences, 34
  tests): baseline file_cards => 34 new cards; this change => 1 intake card,
  35 durable+delivered rows, no duplicates after full replay (also via 32
  real CLI invocations). Cohort = sources of Nov13-created cards, not every
  flake that day. No slot-hour savings claimed until measured live.
- RED->GREEN and behavioral mutants for each fix are recorded on Kanban
  t_01a1d7718abcfb81 (e.g. dropping owner_check notes or re-stranding clipped
  closed candidates fails 11 tests).

Journal mode retires a stored run whose data stays unavailable once a
successful listing no longer includes it and it has reached the FAILED
threshold: one terminal "FAILED ... retired=unlisted" line, then complete.
A failed listing never retires. (Review t_01a1d88fc18f9c02 blocker.)

Rollback: revert this commit. The old per-test producer ignores the queue
file. Keep <state>.intake.sqlite3 and its identity anchor until every
undelivered row has an owner (see docs/ci-flaky-intake.md for backlog export).
